  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "News looks like a plural but is uncountable and takes a singular verb: the news is good, this news. To speak of one item, say a piece of news, a bit of news, or a news item, never a news. New, without the s, is only an adjective.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "I have a good news for you.",
      "correct": "I have some good news for you.",
      "note": "News is uncountable and never takes a or an; for one item say a piece of news."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "The news are bad.",
      "correct": "The news is bad.",
      "note": "News ends in s but is singular and takes a singular verb."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "One English word covers both fresh information you hear from a friend and the news reports of the media; many languages use one word for the first and a different word, often a plural, for the second. Both senses are singular in English.",
    "grammar": "Uncountable and singular though it ends in s: the news is on, this news. Never a news or the news are. For one item say a piece of news. A plural word in the learner's language is the usual source of error.",
    "culture": "In English-speaking countries the news on television and radio is a fixed program at set hours, so people say the six o'clock news. Breaking news means news of something happening right now; fake news means false reports spread on purpose.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "One syllable. In American English the vowel is the oo of food with no y sound before it: nooz. In British English a y sound comes first: nyooz. The final s is a z sound, and the s ending does not make the word plural."
  },
